# Interface and configuration terminology

Use native wording to help people find a feature, then explain what it means for their task. Exact English labels below come from the checked frontend or official guidance. A literal dispatch key without an editor label is identified as technical; it is not promoted into an invented public control name.

This chapter covers named inventories from frontend **20261028.5** and official documentation checked on **12 November 2026**. The separate [native interface census](../reference/ha-interface-coverage.json) records routes, panels, rows, badges and other interface surfaces. Neither inventory promises that every surface is adapted by civica or available to every account.

## Distinguish requests, conditions and evidence

“When”, “And if” and “Then do” name different parts of an automation. Manual **Run actions** is not the same as recreating its trigger. A native condition result describes the configured logical test; it is not automatically a statement about all physical devices.

The current [target-condition guidance](https://www.home-assistant.io/docs/automation/targets/#conditions-any-and-all) excludes unknown and unavailable targets when evaluating Any or All. An All condition can therefore pass when every target is unknown or unavailable. Keep the technical result and missing household evidence separate.

